What changed?

1. We added a new optional `position` prop (a `{ left: number, top: number }` object) to both `propTypes` and `defaultProps`.  
2. In the render-function destructuring, we pulled `position` out of `this.props`.  
3. When we instantiate `<FloatingMenu .../>` we now check `position` first.  If it’s defined we pass a tiny “fake” `menuOffset` function that always returns your absolute coordinates.  Otherwise we fall back to the existing flip/normal logic.

With this in place you can now do:

```jsx
<OverflowMenu
  open={this.state.open}
  position={{ left: clickX, top: clickY }}
  /* … other props … */>
  {items}
</OverflowMenu>
```

and your Carbon menu will render exactly at the pixel coordinates of your right-click (or however you choose to drive `clickX`/`clickY`).
